{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Information.AntisymmetricInvolutionForcesZero","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Information.AntisymmetricInvolutionForcesZero","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Matrix.Basic"],"declarations":3,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Information.AntisymmetricInvolutionForcesZero.not_involutive_of_antisymm_entry_ne_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Information.AntisymmetricInvolutionForcesZero.not_involutive_of_antisymm_entry_ne_zero","kind":"theorem","line":111,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Information/AntisymmetricInvolutionForcesZero.lean#L111-L118","doc":"**Contrapositive, the form the q-GoL claim needs.** A unitary matrix with a\n*nonzero* antisymmetric entry is not an involution. At the substrate\n`Φ(q₀) ≠ 0`, so the q-Toffoli is not one. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Information.AntisymmetricInvolutionForcesZero.entry_eq_zero_of_antisymm_of_involutive_orthogonal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Information.AntisymmetricInvolutionForcesZero.entry_eq_zero_of_antisymm_of_involutive_orthogonal","kind":"theorem","line":93,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Information/AntisymmetricInvolutionForcesZero.lean#L93-L109","doc":"**A unitary involution has no antisymmetric entry.** If `M` is an orthogonal\ninvolution and `M i j = −(M j i)`, then `M i j = 0`.\n\nThis is the q-Toffoli statement: the gate is unitary and its `(6,7)` entry is\nminus its `(7,6)` entry, so if it were also an involution that entry would\nvanish — i.e. `Φ(q) = 0`, which happens only in the classical limit `q = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Information.AntisymmetricInvolutionForcesZero.involutive_orthogonal_transpose_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Information.AntisymmetricInvolutionForcesZero.involutive_orthogonal_transpose_eq","kind":"theorem","line":83,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Information/AntisymmetricInvolutionForcesZero.lean#L83-L91","doc":"**An orthogonal involution is symmetric.** Both `Mᵀ` and `M` are inverses of\n`M`, so they agree. ","signatureChunk":0}]}